// Unit 2 Lesson 6 â€” 30-question bank

::U2L6Q01::Fitness data are {=evidence about activity and response~only wearable readings~class rankings~medical diagnoses}

::U2L6Q02::An activity log should use {=consistent fields and understandable units~random fields each session~only memory~classmate comparisons}

::U2L6Q03::A baseline is {=a starting point for comparison~a final grade~a diagnosis~a guaranteed target}

::U2L6Q04::A trend is {=a general direction across multiple observations~one unusual data point~any device reading~a causal proof}

::U2L6Q05::Quantitative data include {=duration and RPE number~talk-test description only~movement-quality note only~enjoyment only}

::U2L6Q06::Qualitative data include {=talk test and recovery description~minutes only~heart-rate number only~days per week only}

::U2L6Q07::Monitoring helps decide whether to {=maintain, progress, modify, or reduce a plan~copy a classmate~ignore recovery~diagnose illness}

::U2L6Q08::One unusually difficult session should usually prompt {=context and pattern review while preserving safety~an immediate conclusion that the plan failed~automatic progression~class comparison}

::U2L6Q09::Correlation is not causation means {=two changes occurring together do not prove one caused the other~all trends are false~data are useless~one point proves a cause}

::U2L6Q10::Technology readings {=may help but have limitations~are automatically exact~are required for every student~replace interpretation}

::U2L6Q11::Appropriate evidence for progression includes {=consistently manageable activity, good form, and adequate recovery~one easy minute~repeated unsafe technique~persistent excessive difficulty}

::U2L6Q12::Repeated RPE above range and poor recovery support {=reducing or modifying demand~increasing everything~removing recovery~ignoring the log}

::U2L6Q13::A new schedule barrier most directly supports {=modifying the plan to fit available time while preserving the goal~calling the plan a failure~changing to an unrelated goal~maximal intensity}

::U2L6Q14::A warning sign should lead to {=stopping and following the safety procedure~finishing the set first~progressing intensity~ignoring data}

::U2L6Q15::Which is a data point? {=RPE 6 in session 2~RPE decreased across five sessions~a repeated pattern~a general trend}

::U2L6Q16::Which statement is a cautious inference? {=Several lower RPE values may indicate the activity is becoming more manageable.~One low value proves permanent improvement.~Poor sleep caused the high RPE.~The device is always correct.}

::U2L6Q17::Session durations 15,15,20,20 show {=a change in Time~a change in Type~proof of causation~a diagnosis}

::U2L6Q18::A missed session from a schedule conflict is {=relevant context for interpreting frequency~proof of poor fitness~a reason to compare classmates~a heart-rate measure}

::U2L6Q19::An outlier is {=an observation noticeably different from others~the average of every session~a fitness goal~a required wearable}

::U2L6Q20::Useful graph axes could be {=session number horizontally and RPE vertically~student names and rankings~diagnoses and symptoms~unlabeled values}

::U2L6Q21::Maintain is reasonable when {=challenge is appropriate, goal is supported, and safety/recovery are adequate~one session feels easy~warning signs recur~the activity is inaccessible}

::U2L6Q22::Progress should usually change {=one or a limited number of variables purposefully~all variables at once~nothing based on evidence~the fitness goal randomly}

::U2L6Q23::Modify is appropriate when {=access, schedule, equipment, movement quality, or recovery changes~a plan must never change~a friend uses another plan~numbers are higher}

::U2L6Q24::Reduce is appropriate when {=difficulty is excessive, technique is unsafe, or recovery is inadequate~the activity is always manageable~progression is already gradual~the goal is clear}

::U2L6Q25::Personal fitness data should primarily be compared with {=the individual's baseline and goal~the fastest classmate~social media~unrelated activities}

::U2L6Q26::A limitation of a six-session log may be {=the observation period is short~it includes any data~it uses consistent fields~it records context}

::U2L6Q27::Missing optional heart-rate readings mean {=other evidence can still be used while noting the limitation~the entire log is invalid~heart rate must be invented~progression is required}

::U2L6Q28::If RPE falls from 6 to 4 across several equal sessions with good recovery {=a small progression may be considered~maximum intensity is required~causation is proven~recovery can be removed}

::U2L6Q29::The strongest recommendation {=names the evidence, decision, exact FITT variable, principles, and monitoring step~uses one data point only~ignores barriers~assumes devices are exact}

::U2L6Q30::The planâ€“monitorâ€“adjust cycle is {=Plan, Perform, Record, Analyze, Decide, Adjust~Plan, Compare, Diagnose, Maximize~Perform, Ignore, Repeat~Record, Rank, Reward}


